  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 240 AM CDT Wed Aug 26 2026\n\nFOG:\n\nEARLY THIS MORNING...Radiational cooling amidst rich low-level \nmoisture may support patchy fog early this morning generally north \nof a line extending from Great Bend to Newton to Chanute. Widespread \ndense fog appears unlikely given unfavorable northerly flow and \ndense high level clouds moving in from the west.\n\nTHUNDERSTORM CHANCES:\n\nLATE TONIGHT--THURSDAY MORNING...Increasing 850-700mb warm/moist \nadvection in response to another shortwave dropping southeast over \nthe Upper Mississippi Valley may support increasing chances for \nshowers/thunderstorms late tonight over Nebraska and north-central \nKS, with this activity possibly propagating south-southeast Thursday \nmorning for locations mainly west of the Flint Hills. Forcing is \nrather weak, and not all models indicate this activity, so \nconfidence is rather low regarding this scenario panning out. Hence, \nwe only went with 20 PoPs for now late tonight through Thursday AM.\n\nSUNDAY EVENING--MID NEXT WEEK...The upper ridge finally shifts east \nfrom the southwest to the south-central and southeast CONUS by this \nweekend, as various shortwaves gradually dig across the northern and \nwestern CONUS. This pattern change should allow a weak cold front to \nmove in from the north and stall across the region, with the \npotential for increasing off-and-on thunderstorm chances late Sunday \nevening through about mid next week. Stay tuned as we refine \nforecast details.\n\nTEMPERATURES:\n\nDeterministic and ensemble consensus is in good agreement \nsurrounding the return of triple digit to near triple digit heat \nthis weekend through at least early to mid next week, as the stout \nsouthwest CONUS upper ridge shifts east over the south-central and \nsoutheast CONUS. The longevity of this heat wave will be determined \nby how far south a stalled frontal zone reaches. Thinking by late \nlate next week, it's possible this frontal zone will be south of the \nregion, supporting somewhat cooler temperatures by then. Some relief \namidst this incoming heat wave will be lower dewpoints and \nbreezy/gusty south to southwest winds aiding in evaporative effects \non skin.",
